[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
postfix-dane-policy needs a local DNSSEC-validating resolver and trusts its
AD bit (it checks that at startup and answers TEMP if the resolver does
not validate). The base system's local_unbound fits the defaults:

  sysrc local_unbound_enable=YES
  service local_unbound start

Enable the service. It runs as user danepolicy and listens on
/var/run/postfix_dane_policy/policy.sock, reachable for group postfix only:

  sysrc postfix_dane_policy_enable=YES
  service postfix_dane_policy start

Other resolver addresses go into postfix_dane_policy_args, see
postfix-dane-policy(8).

Then put it first in main.cf, before an MTA-STS resolver if you use one:

  smtp_tls_security_level = verify
  smtp_dns_support_level = dnssec
  smtp_tls_policy_maps =
      socketmap:unix:/var/run/postfix_dane_policy/policy.sock:dane,
      socketmap:inet:127.0.0.1:8461:mta-sts
  smtp_tls_CAfile = /usr/local/share/certs/ca-root-nss.crt
  tls_required_enable = no
  smtp_tls_mandatory_protocols = >=TLSv1.2

tls_required_enable = no stops senders from disabling the policy with a
"TLS-Required: no" header. verify/dane-only use the mandatory protocols.

Note: "verify" as the global level defers mail to servers without a
valid CA certificate or without STARTTLS.
EOM
}
{ type: upgrade
  maximum_version: "0.3.0"
  message: <<EOM
0.3.0 defaults to a unix socket and the dedicated user danepolicy.
If postfix_dane_policy_args still points to TCP it keeps working; to switch,
clear postfix_dane_policy_args and use
socketmap:unix:/var/run/postfix_dane_policy/policy.sock:dane in main.cf.
EOM
}
]
